{
  /**
   * A SOLUTION file: it compiles nothing itself and only points at the three
   * real projects.
   *
   * The `compilerOptions` below are a SAFETY BASELINE, not the configuration
   * any of those projects use — none of them `extends` this file, and project
   * references do not inherit compiler options.
   *
   * They exist because a tool that does not understand project references —
   * Vercel's function compiler is one — reads this file, finds it nominally
   * applicable to `api/**` and `server/**`, and then fills every unset option
   * from its own defaults. A bare `{ "paths": … }` handed such a tool a
   * NON-STRICT, environment-less compile, and `strictNullChecks: false`
   * silently stops discriminated unions narrowing through `if (!result.ok)`.
   * That is exactly how a build could pass here and fail there.
   *
   * This is therefore a PRODUCTION COMPILER INPUT, not decoration. Vercel
   * compiles every `api/*.ts` entrypoint on its own against these options,
   * which `pnpm typecheck:vercel-functions` reproduces.
   *
   * WHY `DOM` IS HERE, in a config that also says `types: ["node"]`:
   *
   * Vercel Functions are written against the WEB-standard `Request`,
   * `Response`, `Headers` and `URL` — that is the runtime's real API, and it
   * is not being changed. Without a DOM lib those names come only from
   * `@types/node`'s `web-globals/fetch.d.ts`, which declares them as
   *
   *     type _Request = typeof globalThis extends { onmessage: any }
   *       ? {}                  // assume a DOM lib will supply the real one
   *       : undici.Request      // otherwise use Node's own
   *
   * That heuristic infers "is a DOM lib loaded?" from an unrelated global. Get
   * it wrong — in either direction — and `Request` collapses to `{}`, which
   * is exactly the reported `Property 'headers' does not exist on type
   * 'Request'`. Naming `DOM` explicitly removes the guess: the real Fetch
   * interfaces are always in the program, whatever else a compiler decides
   * about `globalThis`.
   *
   * This is NOT the browser project. `tsconfig.app.json` keeps its own
   * `types: ["vite/client"]` and is untouched by anything here, and server
   * code must still never use `window`, `document` or any other browser-only
   * global — `release:check` enforces that separately, because the fallback
   * environment can no longer catch it.
   */
  "files": [],
  "references": [
    { "path": "./tsconfig.app.json" },
    { "path": "./tsconfig.node.json" },
    { "path": "./tsconfig.server.json" }
  ],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"]
    },
    "strict": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "target": "es2023",
    "lib": ["ES2023", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "module": "nodenext",
    "moduleResolution": "nodenext",
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true
  }
}
